langen:dwave 0, "voice\53\75300543.ogg":^"The first character in Kirigan has a 口, the character for mouth.^@:dwave 0, "voice\53\75300544.ogg":^  And 其, the other half of that first character, means the word `that', right...?!^@:dwave 0, "voice\53\75300545.ogg":^  `That village's shore', and the mouth is `telling of' it...!^@/
advsetwindow "53"

;ld r,RIO_KomaruA1,80

langjp　……ん、…でも、“二人が”口にし、の意味がわからない…。」\
;＜理御
langen:dwave 0, "voice\53\75300546.ogg":^  ...Ah, ...but I don't get what `the two' means..."^\
advsetwindow "-1"

langjp　キリガンを意味する３文字の漢字は、里、口、其、岸、などで構成されている。@
langen^The three characters of Kirigan can be split into a mouth (口), a `that' (其), a village (里), and a shore (岸).^@
advsetwindow "-1"
;br
langjp　それらは何れも、碑文の中に登場する文言だ。\
langen^Those are all words that appear in the epitaph.^\
advsetwindow "-1"

langjp“其の里にて二人が口にし岸を探れ”。@
langen^`In *that* *village*, look for the *shore* the two will *tell* you of.'^@
advsetwindow "-1"
;br
langjp　この駅の名を、暗示しているようにも読み取れる。@
langjp　しかし、二人が口にし、というのがわからない……。\
langen^You can read it as a code for the name of the station.^@
advsetwindow "-1"
langen^However, I don't understand the part about `the two'...^\

clr_text_bg

